ds the initiative as Black\'s stones are not connected and pose no immediate threat.\n2.  **White\'s Opportunities:**\n    *   **Diagonal Line:** White has a diagonal connection with **G9** and **H8**. This is a "Live 2".\n    *   **Vertical/Horizontal:** White has H8-H9 (blocked by H10) and G9-H9 (blocked by F9). These are "Sleep 2" lines and are less effective.\n3.  **Candidate Moves:** The most promising strategy is to extend the diagonal "Live 2" (G9-H8) into an "Open 3" (Live 3). The two ends of this line are **I7** and **F10**.\n    *   **I7:** Creates an Open 3 (G9-H8-I7). It is located in a relatively open area (top-right), with only one opponent stone (I8) adjacent. It allows for clear follow-up attacks.\n    *   **F10:** Also creates an Open 3 (H8-G9-F10). However, it extends into the bottom-left area where Black has stones at F9 and H10. This area is more congested, which might aid Black\'s defense or counter-play.\n4.  **Selection:** **I7** is the superior choice. It forces Black to defend against the immediate Open 3 threat (potential to become an Open 4). By playing into the open space, White maintains better control and potential for future link-ups (e.g., with H9 or vertical expansion), whereas F10 plays into the opponent\'s sphere of influence.\n\nEvaluation: White has a diagonal "Live 2" at G9-H8. Converting this into a "Live 3" (Open 3) is a forcing move that puts Black on the defensive.\n\nReason: The move at I7 extends the diagonal line (G9, H8) to form a 3-in-a-row with open ends (Open 3). This is a strong attacking move that demands an immediate response from Black. I7 is preferred over the alternative F10 because it develops the line into a less crowded area of the board, maximizing future attacking potential.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "I", "row": 7 }'}
