 Crucially, H7 also connects horizontally with I7 to form an "Open 2" (H7-I7), which has space on both sides (G7, J7) to become an Open 3. This move creates intersecting potential lines (Vertical and Horizontal), effectively building a strong "L" shape base for future attacks (Forking).\n    *   **I9:** Playing I9 creates a horizontal line G9-H9-I9 and a diagonal connection H8-I9. Similar to H7, but H7 feels more central and keeps the stones connected to I7.\n    *   **F10:** Creates a 4-in-a-row (F10-G9-H8-I7), forcing Black to play E11. This is a valid tactic to keep initiative, but without a follow-up VCF (Victory by Continuous Fours), it might waste a potential threat. H7 is a more constructive "building" move.\n\n**Reason:**\nThe move at H7 is the most strategic choice. It extends an existing vertical pair into a 3-in-a-row (H7-H8-H9) and simultaneously connects with I7 to form a horizontal Open 2 (H7-I7). This creates multiple avenues for attack (Vertical and Horizontal) and strengthens White\'s control of the center. It sets up a double threat: threatening to make a 4-in-a-row vertically (by playing H6) and an Open 3 horizontally (by playing J7).\n\nAnswer:\n{\n  "col": "H",\n  "row": 7\n}'}
