ed analysis, again going through the board state, analyzing stones. I finally realized that H11 (row 11, column H) is the only move I could see that formed an Open 3. I spent time making sure I had the right line.\n\nI re-examined the line to see if it was blocked by any black stones, and also to see if there were any side connections. If white plays H11, it is the only viable move I\'ve been able to find. It creates the only "Open 3" – a diagonal line with the stones J9, I10, and H11. There are spaces at G12 and K8 that could be extended to make a win. But, Black may try to prevent a win. Black\'s stones in the range are the main concern.\n\nI have verified all the possible ways black may disrupt my "Open 3" strategy at H11.\n\nI have spent an extensive time evaluating the situation. If there is a winning move, I have not found it.\n\nIn the end, I concluded that H11, while not immediately guaranteeing a win, is the most strategic option available.\n\nSo, H11. That\'s the move.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: White has a strong presence in the center but many of its 4-in-a-row lines are blocked at both ends (e.g., Row 7, Col H, Diagonals F10-I7, G5-J8, H9-K12, M5-J8). White must find a move that creates a new threat. The most promising pattern is the diagonal connection between J9 and I10.\n\nReason: The move at H11 creates a diagonal 3-in-a-row with stones J9, I10, and H11. This line has open spaces at both ends (K8 and G12). Specifically, the line is L7(X)-K8(Empty)-J9(O)-I10(O)-H11(New)-G12(Empty)-F13(Empty). Creating this "Open 3" forces Black to respond (likely by blocking at K8 or G12), allowing White to maintain the initiative. Black has a potential threat to extend their I9-J10-K11 line to L12, but this results in a blocked 4 (stopped by M13) and is not immediately lethal, giving White time to attack first.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 11}'}
