es that I12 is the right move for White.\n\nI double-checked to see that it is an available move in the board, and no Black move can be a counter. It connects stones in the center, potentially setting up an open line. It also connects the middle of the board.\n\nFinal decision. I\'ll provide I12 as the move.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The board is heavily congested, and most of White\'s obvious lines (like the H6-H9 vertical, G5-J8 diagonal, and others) are \'dead\' lines blocked on both ends by Black stones. White needs to find a fresh line of attack. The diagonal line extending from G10 to K14 contains the White stone at H11 and is bounded by F9(X) on the top-left, allowing for exactly 5 stones (G10-K14). This seems to be the only potential \'live\' line.\n\nReason: Playing at I12 connects with H11 to form a "Live 2" on the G10-K14 diagonal. If ignored, White can play J13 next to form a 3-in-a-row (H11-I12-J13) which threatens to complete the 5-stone line. Additionally, I12 connects structurally with other White stones at H12 and J11, solidifying the center and filling a gap. Black does not appear to have any immediate winning threats that require blocking.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 12}'}
